# kantainer machine configuration — EXAMPLE
#
# Copy this file, fill in your own values, and keep your copy:
#
#     cp kantainer.conf.example kantainer.conf
#     $EDITOR kantainer.conf
#     just config-check
#
# This is the only place machine-specific values live (SPEC.md
# §spec:machine-configuration). Everything else belongs in the image. Keep the
# filled-in copy — regenerating a USB stick needs it.
#
# kantainer.conf is git-ignored and MUST STAY OUT of this repository. The
# repository is public and your copy carries a password in plain text
# (REQUIREMENTS.md §req:quality-attributes).
#
# FORMAT — one KEY=value per line. The value is everything after the first `=`,
# taken literally to the end of the line. No quotes, no escapes, no shell
# expansion: a password containing $ ` " ' \ or spaces is written exactly as it
# reads. Trailing spaces are part of the value, so do not add any. Blank lines
# and lines starting with # are ignored.


### Required

# The login account created on the machine. A Linux user name: lower-case
# letters, digits, underscore and dash, starting with a letter or underscore.
KANTAINER_USERNAME=

# The SSH PUBLIC key that account accepts — one line, the whole contents of
# your .pub file. This is the only way in: the machine refuses password logins
# for every account (SPEC.md §spec:remote-access). Never put the private key
# here.
#
#     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
#
# Shape: ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz...  you@example
KANTAINER_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY=

# The Portainer administrator password, at least 12 characters — the minimum
# Portainer accepts without forcing a change at first login.
#
# Required, not optional. Current Portainer will not create its first
# administrator without a token it prints only to its own log, so leaving this
# blank means you cannot claim the account from a browser at all (SPEC.md
# §spec:machine-configuration).
KANTAINER_PORTAINER_PASSWORD=


### Optional

# The password for logging in at the machine's OWN KEYBOARD, at least 12
# characters. Leave it blank and no account has a password at all — which is
# what this machine is without this line.
#
# Leaving it blank is a supported choice, not a mistake. It costs you this: if
# this machine's network ever fails, you cannot reach it at all, and reflashing
# is the only way back in.
#
# It never reaches the network. SSH refuses password logins for every account
# whether or not you set this (SPEC.md §spec:remote-access), so it widens
# physical access and nothing else.
#
# Twelve characters is a floor because the login prompt is the whole of the
# gate: once past it, privileged commands do not ask again.
KANTAINER_CONSOLE_PASSWORD=

# The drive to install to, e.g. /dev/sda or /dev/nvme0n1. Leave blank on a
# single-drive machine. On a machine with more than one drive, leaving this
# blank means the installer stops and asks rather than erasing one
# (SPEC.md §spec:drive-selection).
KANTAINER_TARGET_DRIVE=

# Wireless network. Leave BOTH blank on a wired machine — a wired machine
# carries no wireless configuration at all, and wired DHCP needs none. Set
# both or neither (SPEC.md §spec:network-attachment).
#
# Installation itself is always wired, even for a machine that will run on
# wireless afterwards: the installer is stock Fedora CoreOS and has no wireless
# support. Install on ethernet once, then move the machine.
KANTAINER_WIFI_SSID=

# The WPA passphrase for that network, 8–63 characters.
KANTAINER_WIFI_PASSPHRASE=

# Whether the machine runs Watchtower, which updates the containers you deploy
# (SPEC.md §spec:container-updates). `true` or `false`; blank means false.
#
# Watchtower is carried inside the image either way. This line only decides
# whether it STARTS. Leaving it off costs you nothing you cannot change later:
#
#     sudo touch /etc/kantainer/watchtower-enabled
#     sudo systemctl start kantainer-watchtower
#
# WHAT SETTING IT TO true MEANS. Once a night at 05:00 UTC, Watchtower checks
# the containers you have LABELLED for it, and any whose image has a newer
# version is stopped, pulled and recreated, with the old image deleted. Nobody is
# watching when it happens, and a container that breaks on its new version stays
# broken until you look.
#
# Nothing is updated until you opt a container in, in the stack that defines it:
#
#     labels:
#       com.centurylinklabs.watchtower.enable: "true"
#
# So switching this on and labelling nothing changes nothing. A container you
# forget to label is simply never updated.
#
# It holds the Docker socket, which is root on this machine. See
# docs/watchtower.md before switching it on - including how to change the
# schedule, add notifications, or have it update every container instead.
KANTAINER_WATCHTOWER_ENABLED=
